menu "Kernel"

config PROCESSORS_MAX
    int "Maximum number of CPUs"
    default 8

config TIMER_HZ
    int "Timer tick rate (Hz)"
    default 1000
    help
      Frequency of the kernel timer tick in Hz. This controls the
      scheduling granularity and timeslice length.

        1000 = 1 ms tick (default, good for interactive/debug)
         250 = 4 ms tick (lower overhead)
         100 = 10 ms tick (traditional Unix)

config SMP_MSGOPT
    bool "SMP lockless message transfer optimization"
    default y
    depends on SMP
    help
      Allow cross-CPU message transfers without holding the kernel lock.
      Threads in mid-transfer are marked with RTMUX_ITF_MSG_DELIVERY so
      other CPUs skip them.

      Default flipped to y in P2.1e (May 2026) — the IPC calls are
      transitioning off the BKL (the "inkernel lock relaxed" condition
      the option was waiting for).  See arch/riscv/calls.c's
      kcall_no_bkl[] table and kernel/ipc/fastmsg.c::kcall_msg_receivev
      for the consumers.

config INSTRUMENTATION
    bool "Kernel instrumentation (trace events)"
    default n
    help
      Enable the kernel instrumentation framework for trace event
      logging.  Used by tracelogger and performance analysis tools.

config STALL_DETECTOR
    bool "Kernel stall detector (boot-hang watchdog)"
    default n
    help
      Add a lightweight watchdog that fires from clock_handler when
      roottask's heartbeat stops advancing for more than ~15 seconds.
      On fire, dumps per-CPU active thread (pid/tid/state/pc/ra/sp/
      syscall/blocked_on) + every process's thread states + the
      inkernel lock value, then disarms.

      Useful for debugging silent daemon-spawn hangs where the kernel
      deadlocks without any crash output.  Runs on every CPU's clock
      ISR *before* the in_kernel_context() bailout, so it still fires
      when the current CPU is stuck inside a kernel critical section.
      a short check per timer tick.  Safe to leave on; turn off when
      chasing microbenchmark regressions or shipping a release build.

      Also enables the per-hart neighbour watchdog (kernel/debug/
      hartwatch.c): every hart watches the next one's timer-interrupt
      counter and logs one "hartwatch:" line naming a hart that has
      taken no timer interrupt for 2 seconds.  Unlike the heartbeat
      check it does not depend on the boot hart's clock.

config MONITORING_KTHREAD
    bool "Dedicate the last hart to a kernel monitoring thread"
    default n
    help
      Reserve the last hart for a dedicated monitoring kthread.  The
      kthread runs in S-mode on that hart, never enters the dispatcher,
      never takes any kspinlock, and is invisible to RTMux: from the
      scheduler's perspective the system has (num_cpu - 1) harts.

      The thread snapshots the state of every global kspinlock in the
      kernel once per second and prints the result via the SBI console.
      Its purpose is post-mortem visibility into stuck-lock conditions:
      because it never participates in any locking, it cannot itself
      be wedged by a kernel deadlock and its prints continue to flow
      even when the rest of the system is silent.

      With -smp 4 in QEMU, enabling this means RTMux runs on 3 CPUs and
      the 4th hart is the monitor.

menu "Debugging"

config DEBUG_INJECT
    bool "Debug fault-injection private call"
    default n
    help
      Build the DEBUG_INJECT private call (RTMUX_PRIV_CAP_INJECT): stall or
      hang another hart on demand, and make the next allocation from a
      chosen kernel pool fail once. Lets tests produce a deterministic fault
      instead of waiting for a race; used by the hang-hunting harness.

      Off in the default configuration: the call is unbound (ENOSYS) and no
      code is built. Never enable it on a deployed system.

config LOCK_DEBUG
    bool "Kernel lock debug (lock-rank checker)"
    default n
    depends on SMP
    help
      Track every fine-grained kernel lock as it is acquired and
      released, and verify that the acquisition order matches the
      static rank declared by its LOCK_CLASS_*.  Detects cycles
      (potential deadlocks) at runtime and flags rank inversions.

      Pure correctness aid — adds per-acquire bookkeeping and is meant
      for development builds, not production.

config TRACE_SYSCALLS
    bool "Trace all syscalls (very verbose)"
    default n
    help
      Print every syscall (number, name, a0-a2) to the kernel console,
      both from U-mode ecalls and S-mode __kcall.  Extremely verbose —
      use only for debugging the syscall dispatch path.

config TRACE_ROOTTASK_MSGS
    bool "Trace roottask IPC message dispatch (very verbose)"
    default n
    help
      Print every message/notification received by roottask's dispatch loop,
      and every reply sent via proc_status().  Shows rcvid, pid, tid,
      message type on receive; rcvid and status on reply.
      Extremely verbose — use only for debugging the IPC dispatch path.

config TRACE_RING
    bool "Per-CPU function-entry trace ring"
    default n
    help
      Enable the low-level per-CPU trace ring used to reconstruct recent
      kernel function entries when a crash path calls trace_ring_dump().
      Instrumented call sites must use TRACE_FN().

config UMODE_VERBOSE_FAULT_REPORT
    bool "Verbose user-space fault report"
    default n
    help
      On a fatal user-space fault, dump a rich diagnostic before the
      process is torn down: the exact scause, the page-table state of
      both the fault IP and the fault address, the full register file,
      and a fault-safe scan of the user stack for candidate return
      addresses.  When disabled (the default) only the one-line
      "user fault: pid=... ip=..." summary is printed.

      Useful for hunting memory corruption / control-flow faults (e.g.
      the FU740 I-cache investigation).  Costs nothing at runtime when
      off; the dump itself only runs on an already-fatal fault.

config DEBUG_PRIL
    bool "Priority list debugging"
    default n
    help
      Use function calls instead of inline macros for pril_first/pril_next,
      enabling breakpoints and tracing in the priority list code.

config DEBUG_VECTOR
    bool "Vector table validation"
    default n
    help
      Enable extra validation in the kernel vector table operations.

config COUNT_CYCLES
    bool "CPU cycle counting"
    default n

config PSP_STARTUP
    bool "Print syspage during startup"
    default n

config TRACKPMEM
    bool "Track physical memory allocations"
    default n
    help
      Enable allocation tracking in the physical memory allocator.

config FDT_DEBUG
    bool "FDT (device tree) debug output"
    default n

config KPRINTF_MAX_LIMIT
    int "kprintf maximum output length"
    default 256

config KPRINTF_PAD_HEX_WITH_ZEROS
    bool "Pad hex values with zeros"
    default y

config MAX_BOOTLINE_SIZE
    int "Maximum boot command line size"
    default 256

endmenu
endmenu
